 @TopShot501st  To be honest prices can still be high even if you are only supplier: when you sell something at such a price that it's simply not worth the buy (a.k.a. you are better not buying it at all).

In case of those graphic cards it's not the case. 2060 in particular is still a good value. Everything else is kind of high end, but if you have a money to spend for a small boost in performance, those are also fine purchases.

Tbh the sup 1000$ build with 2060 and Ryzen 3600 will be able to handle most games on high and ultra setting at the very least at 1080p with 60+ FPS for the nearest few years, so unless you are a PC enthusiast, there is no reason to pay more.

 @siintrovert5343  You could've gotten a GTX1080 for $499 two years ago. 

Besides, why pay $399 for GTX1080 performance when there's a $299 card that performs akin to a GTX1080? Vega56 is still better bang for buck than the RTX2060 or 2060S.
Ever since Vega56 dropped at/below $300, there has been zero progress in the mid-range section. 90% of the people watching this don't own $500 GPU's.

 @Malus1531  Hmm, that's weird, what CPU are you using? Maybe your graphic card is bottlenecked?

Or maybe Anno 1800 is just poorly optimized. I mean, just looking at the tested games, none of the games has 2060 super 1% lowest at less than 60 FPS, except Assassin's Creed Odyssey, and that one behaved like that with all graphic cards. So unless poor optimization is involved, this shouldn't ever happen.

But maybe you are right, I don't own a PC at the moment, and my experience is solely based on different reviews and benchmarks I saw online, and I didn't see many of those where 2060 performed worse than 60 average fps, and 2060 super is close to 2070, so there should there shouldn't be any issues.

 @WitchYuki22  Well, I guess you can't run games at 144 FPS on ultra on 2060 graphic card, but pretty sure it will be able to handle most titles at 60+ FPS with some graphic settings tweaking even after 3 years, and 3 years is a pretty long lifespan for a graphic card. 1080 Ti is 3 years old, and still performs on par with 2080 Ti, with the exception of Ray Tracing, and can run all modern titles on ultra. I guess you might want a 2060 super after all for those 8 Gb memory, but if you aren't really concerned about running everything on ultra, then 2060 is still a sweet spot.

It will most certainly be more than capable of handling any cybersport related tytles at best possible performance, and will still provide a satisfying experience with future games.

Definitely not worth going for anything past 2070 Super on budget builds, even 2070 Super is questionable in terms of cost efficiency.

